=== Cursor Memory Bounding Demo (pycubrid 1.6+) ===

[setup] Seeded 5000 rows into cookbook_fetch_probe

Comparing peak client memory for a full table scan of 5000 wide rows:

  fetch_size=   10  peak_memory={{MEM}} KB  (fetchmany(10) loop)
  fetch_size=  100  peak_memory={{MEM}} KB  (fetchmany(10) loop)
  fetch_size= 1000  peak_memory={{MEM}} KB  (fetchmany(10) loop)
  fetchall()        peak_memory={{MEM}} KB  (fetch_size=100, all rows kept)

Peak memory ratio (largest / smallest): {{RATIO}}x

Key takeaway: peak client memory follows the rows you keep. Reading in
fetchmany() batches stays near one batch, while fetchall() holds the
whole result. fetch_size sets how many rows each round-trip brings, so
smaller pages mean more round-trips; compare the fetchmany() lines above
to see how much the page size itself adds on your system.

--- fetch_size vs arraysize ---
  fetch_size: server page size (connection-level, set via connect())
  arraysize:  client batch size for cursor.fetchmany() (per-cursor)
  They are INDEPENDENT. fetch_size governs network/memory paging;
  arraysize governs how many rows fetchmany() returns per call.
